  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\n\n...UPDATED AVIATION DISCUSSION...\n\nWeak offshore flow and weak ridging aloft will continue through the \nmiddle of the week. These conditions will maintain mostly clear \nskies and above average temperatures. Highs will be 7 to 15 degrees \nabove average, with the greatest departure from normal along the \ncoast. \n\nOn Thursday, A low pressure system moving toward the California \ncoast will weaken high pressure aloft with lower-level flow becoming \nonshore and strengthening. That will bring cooler conditions that \nwill be maintained through the weekend and the return of onshore \nflow. There is low confidence in when any marine layer low clouds or \nfog would return, but they're expected as early as Thursday or \nFriday. \n\nThere is still a lot of uncertainty around the track and timing of \nthe aforementioned low pressure system and the impacts it may bring \nto Southern California this weekend. Current forecast has periods of \nrain chances starting Friday evening and continuing into Saturday. \nThere is still quite a bit of spread in potential rainfall amounts \nwith some ensemble members remaining dry and other showing around an \ninch of rain. NBM chances of rainfall totals exceeding 0.5 inch \nbetween Friday evening and Saturday evening are 15-25%, with the \nhighest chances on the coastal mountain slopes. It's also possible \nthat winds will increase over the mountains and deserts as the low \npasses Friday night into Saturday. \n\nGlobal ensembles keep a troughing pattern over the West Coast \ncontinuing Sunday into early next week. This will continue our \ncooler, cloudier conditions with additional chances of \nprecipitation. There is low confidence in any details of potential \nrainfall for early next week.",
